e("1799-02","Early February 1799 (Sha'ban–Ramadan 1213)","month","abutaleb","Calcutta","A burned booking, then a Danish ship",
 "He first took a passage on the Company ship Charlotte, which caught fire and burned a few days later. He then took a cabin on the Danish ship Christiana, which Stewart's translation names under Captain 'Netdeman'. Richardson had promised to teach him English on the way and to see to his expenses.",
 "","approx",TRAV+"; summarised in Passages, 'Mirza Abu Talib Khan: notes and sources'.")
e("1799-02-07","Thursday 7 February 1799 (≈2 Ramadan 1213 AH)","day","abutaleb","Hooghly river below Calcutta","Sailing for Europe with Captain Richardson",
 "He left Calcutta with his Scottish friend Captain David Richardson, aged about forty-six and with no post or pension. He had lost office in Awadh and waited years in Calcutta for an appointment that never came.",
 "","verified","Departure date in Banglapedia, 'Khan, Mirza Abu Taleb', and Wikipedia; Hijri date computed (tabular).")
e("1799-02-07","7 February 1799 (as recalled in 1810)","day","williamson","Bengal, aboard ship","A fellow passenger remembers the voyage",
 "Williamson wrote eleven years later that the Mirza had embarked in the same ship as himself from Bengal. He gave this as the basis for claiming a close view of the man's character. The claim is his own and is not confirmed by Abu Taleb's account as summarised in the sources consulted.",
 "","approx","Williamson, East India Vade-Mecum (1810) i.503, via Pritchett's notes.")
e("1799-03","February–March 1799","range","abutaleb","Nicobar Islands, Bay of Bengal","A first landfall among the Nicobar islanders",
 "The ship touched at the Andaman and Nicobar Islands on the way south. This was his first sight of a people outside the Persianate and British worlds he knew, and he described them for his readers at home.",
 "","approx","Live History India summary of the Travels; exact dates not checked in the translation.")
e("1799-06","Mid-1799 (Muharram–Rabi' I 1214)","range","abutaleb","Cape Town, Cape of Good Hope","Table Mountain and a British garrison town",
 "At the Cape, then held by Britain, he stayed some months. He admired Table Mountain and moved in the Cape social circle of Lady Anne Barnard. He reports that the Danish captain and crew had plundered an abandoned Company vessel that had caught fire, and that the authorities caught up with them at the Cape. That ended his voyage in the Christiana.",
 "","approx","Banglapedia; Wikipedia (Barnard circle; stay of some months); Live History India (Table Mountain).")
e("1799-09","Late September 1799","month","abutaleb","Cape Town","Changing to a South Sea whaler for London",
 "Stranded by the Danish ship's troubles, he and Richardson took passage on a South Sea whaler bound for London. In wartime every sail sighted had to be judged friend or foe.",
 "","approx","Wikipedia (departure late September 1799); Banglapedia (whaler).")
e("1799-10","October–November 1799","range","abutaleb","St Helena, South Atlantic","Calling at St Helena",
 "The whaler called at the Company island of St Helena, his last stop before Europe.",
 "","approx","Live History India summary; dates not checked.")
e("1799-12","Early December 1799 (Rajab 1214)","range","abutaleb","Cork, Ireland","Ireland: the first European port",
 "His first European landfall was Cork. Captain Baker, an acquaintance from India, took him to his estate a few miles from the city. He called the place 'a terrestrial paradise'. Baker's kitchen was 'the first regular kitchen' he had seen. He also wrote that the Irish villages much resembled those of India.",
 "a terrestrial paradise","verified","'From Cork to Dublin' chapter, 1814 edition, TOTA reprint (tota.world/article/472); Banglapedia (Cork as first port).")
e("1799-12-08","Sunday 8 December 1799 (≈10 Rajab 1214 AH)","day","abutaleb","Cork to Dublin by mail coach","By mail coach across Munster and Leinster",
 "Having booked places at three guineas each, he and his party set out for Dublin by mail coach. The road ran through Fermoy, Clonmel, Kilkenny and Carlow. The chapter compares what he saw with places in India, Rohilkhand and Murshidabad among them.",
 "","verified","'From Cork to Dublin', 1814 edition (TOTA reprint), which gives the date of 8 December.")
e("1799-12","December 1799","month","abutaleb","Dublin","Leinster House, Colonel Wombell and Astley's circus",
 "In Dublin he was invited to the Duke of Leinster's house, which he thought the most superb in the city, and Colonel Wombell paid him attentions. He was 'much entertained' by Philip Astley's company of trick riders. He records that the troupe spent four or five months a year in Dublin. He went to Dublin partly to call on Lord Cornwallis, the Lord Lieutenant. Richardson went on to London ahead of him.",
 "much entertained by an exhibition of Horsemanship","verified","1814 edition, quoted in Mark Dominus's review (blog.plover.com, 2006); Banglapedia (Cornwallis; Richardson going ahead).")
e("1800-01","December 1799–January 1800","range","abutaleb","Dublin","The Irish: hospitable, witty, imprudent",
 "Summing up the Irish, he called them less intolerant than the English and free of Scots austerity and bigotry. They were hospitable and open-hearted, he wrote, though lacking in prudence, and 'witty, and quick of comprehension'. His landlady and her children understood his broken English. His Irish friends warned him that the English would not take the trouble to understand him.",
 "witty, and quick of comprehension","verified","1814 edition, quoted in Dominus's review (blog.plover.com).")
e("1800-01-21","Tuesday 21 January 1800 (≈24 Sha'ban 1214 AH)","day","abutaleb","London","Arrival in London",
 "He crossed from Ireland to Britain and reached London. Cornwallis's letter of recommendation opened doors at court and to ministers, and he soon took lodgings. Williamson later located them in Gresse Street, off Rathbone Place.",
 "","verified","Arrival date: Banglapedia and Wikipedia. Route via Holyhead from the general account; Gresse Street from Williamson (1810).")
e("1800-02","Early 1800","range","abutaleb","St James's, London","Presented at court; the Queen asks him back",
 "Through Cornwallis he was presented to King George III and Queen Charlotte. He records that the Queen commanded him 'frequently to court'. Within weeks, he says, noble households were competing for him as a guest.",
 "frequently to court","approx","Stewart translation p. 80, as cited by Amrit Sen, 'The Persian Prince in London', Asiatic 2.1 (2008); exact date not given.")
e("1800-06","Summer 1800","range","abutaleb","Vauxhall Gardens, London","The crowd at Vauxhall crowns him 'Persian Prince'",
 "When word went round that he would appear at Vauxhall, an unusually large crowd gathered to look at him. The public called him the Persian Prince. He insisted he had never sought or claimed the title.",
 "","approx","Stewart translation p. 96, cited by Sen (2008); the summer season is inferred, as Vauxhall opened in summer.")
e("1800-07-07","Monday 7 July 1800","day","morningpost","London","The Duchess of Devonshire's gala breakfast",
 "The Morning Post gave its readers two reports, on 7 and 8 July, of the Duchess of Devonshire's gala breakfast. This was one of the great summer entertainments of Whig society, the circle that had taken up the Mirza. The reports describe the setting, the company and the display, the kind of occasion his Travels describes from the inside.",
 "","approx","Broadview edition, Appendix A.2 (Morning Post and Gazetteer, 7 and 8 July 1800). Whether and how he is named was not checked in the original.")
e("1800","1800–1802","range","abutaleb","London","Banks's Sundays, the Indian Club, routs of four hundred",
 "He lists a crowded social calendar. He went to Sir Joseph Banks's Sunday meetings of Royal Society men, dined at the London Tavern with the Indian Club, and attended a musical society at Lady Charlotte's house. He saw Walker's great orrery several times, went to masquerades, and attended routs with three or four hundred guests. He noticed both the crush and the conventions, and he recorded a guest's mock complaint about noise at a masquerade.",
 "","verified","1814 edition, London entertainments chapter, TOTA reprint (tota.world/article/910). Undated within the stay.")
e("1800","1800","year","abutaleb","London","An ode to London in the manner of Hafiz",
 "He wrote a Persian ode in praise of London, modelled on Hafiz, whose divan he had edited in Calcutta. It set the tone of his celebrity: a poet from the East paying compliments to the capital.",
 "","approx","Sen (2008), citing Stewart translation p. 70; Iranica on his Hafiz edition (Calcutta 1791).")
e("1800","1800–1801","range","abutaleb","London","A broken statue sold for five thousand pounds",
 "He was present when a classical statue without head, arms or legs sold for £5,000, which he reckoned at 40,000 rupees. He used the sale to show how highly the Europeans, and especially the French and Italians, valued ancient sculpture.",
 "","approx","1814 edition, quoted in Dominus's review (blog.plover.com); exact occasion undated.")
e("1800-11-10","Monday 10 November 1800 (≈22 Jumada II 1215 AH)","day","abutaleb","Guildhall, City of London","At the high table with Nelson",
 "At the Lord Mayor's banquet he was seated at the high table near Lord Nelson. Guests bowed and curtsied to Nelson and to him as well. He took this as respect for the high rank people supposed he held, part of the 'prince' misunderstanding.",
 "","approx","Stewart translation p. 133, cited by Sen (2008). Date from the Oracle's report of the 1800 feast (Broadview Appendix A.3); the Travels' own date was not checked.")
e("1800-11-11","Tuesday 11 November 1800","day","oracle","London","The Oracle reports the Lord Mayor's Feast",
 "The Oracle published an account of the Lord Mayor's Feast at Guildhall. Nelson, newly home from Naples, was the City's hero that night. The modern editor reprints it beside the Mirza's description of the same evening, so the two can be read together: the City's view of its own pageant, and an Indian guest's view of the bowing.",
 "","approx","Broadview edition, Appendix A.3, 'The Lord Mayor's Feast', Oracle and Daily Advertiser (11 November 1800). Contents of the report not checked.")
e("1801","c. 1801","year","northcote","London","Northcote paints the Mirza",
 "Abu Taleb sat to several London artists, and the Royal Academician James Northcote painted him. The portrait shows him half-length in turban and robes. A mezzotint after it, dated about 1801, carried the image beyond the studio.",
 "","approx","Royal Academy collection: mezzotint after Northcote, dated '1801?'; Banglapedia (six portraits, one by Northcote).")
e("1801","1801","year","abutaleb","London","Lady Spencer asks for candour",
 "Lavinia, Countess Spencer, one of his most attentive hostesses, urged him to publish an account of his travels. She asked him to say frankly what he disliked in English customs as well as what he admired.",
 "","approx","Stewart translation p. 144, cited by Sen (2008).")
e("1801","1801","year","abutaleb","London","The Bishop of London's question",
 "The Bishop of London asked him whether he had come to convert the English to Islam. He recorded the question as an example of what his hosts assumed about a Muslim visitor.",
 "","approx","Stewart translation p. 153, cited by Sen (2008).")
e("1801","1801","year","abutaleb","London","Answering the drawing rooms on Asian women",
 "His hostesses kept telling him that the women of Asia had no liberty. He answered in a Persian essay that took six supposed restrictions in turn and argued each was custom, prudence or protection rather than bondage. The six were seclusion, polygamy, the husband's power of divorce, the weight given to women's testimony, widows' restraint and arranged marriage. He then listed eight advantages Asian women held, from control of the household purse to custody of daughters.",
 "the women of Asia have no liberty at all","verified","'Vindication of the Liberties of the Asiatic Women', Asiatic Annual Register for 1801, Misc. Tracts pp. 100–107 (text via Turcomanica). The quote is the opinion he sets out to refute.")
e("1801","Volume for 1801 (issued 1802)","year","asiaticreg","London","The Register prints the 'Vindication'",
 "The Asiatic Annual Register printed the essay in English among its Miscellaneous Tracts. It opens by restating the European claim, before its six answers and eight counter-advantages. Placed among Company documents and Indian news, it was a rare piece of Asian argument addressed directly to British readers.",
 "the little intercourse with men, and concealment from view","verified","Asiatic Annual Register 1801, Misc. Tracts pp. 100–107 (Turcomanica transcription). Issue date of the volume approximate.")
e("1802-06-07","Monday 7 June 1802 (≈5 Safar 1217 AH)","day","abutaleb","London to Dover","Leaving London in the Peace",
 "With the Peace of Amiens open, he left London for France and the overland road home. He carried a letter of introduction from Lord Pelham. Richardson stayed behind, and the plan of a Persian teaching post in England had come to nothing.",
 "","verified","Banglapedia and Wikipedia (7 June 1802); Banglapedia (Pelham's letter).")
e("1802-06","June 1802","month","abutaleb","Paris","Paris after London",
 "He judged Paris much inferior to London: dirtier, more cramped and badly lit, with lazy waiters and servants. Its people, he thought, suffered from inertia and want of exertion. He liked the Tuileries' statues and fountains, French sweets and French politeness, which he set against the irritable and surly Englishman. Leaving London, he said, felt like falling from paradise into hell.",
 "","approx","Scroll.in essay on the Travels (paraphrasing Stewart); TehranBureau summary. The Paris section is not dated to the day.")
e("1802-07","Summer 1802","range","abutaleb","Louvre, Paris","The Grand Gallery outshines the British Museum",
 "The Louvre's Grand Gallery, newly filled with paintings and statues brought from Italy, struck him as a magnificent room with an abundance of light. Beside it, London's collections seemed children's playthings. It was one of the few points on which France beat England in his ledger.",
 "","approx","Scroll.in, quoting Stewart's translation.")
e("1802-07","Summer 1802","range","abutaleb","Paris","Orientalists, and an audience missed",
 "In Paris he met the orientalists Louis-Mathieu Langlès, keeper of oriental manuscripts at the national library, and Antoine-Isaac Silvestre de Sacy. He was invited to meet Talleyrand and the First Consul but fell ill and could not go. Colonel Wombell, whom he had met in Dublin, turned up again, and a Mr and Miss Ogilvy were attentive.",
 "","approx","Banglapedia (Langlès, de Sacy, Talleyrand and Napoleon, illness); Dominus review (Wombell, Ogilvys).")
e("1802-10","Autumn 1802","range","abutaleb","Lyons and northern Italy","Italy changes his mind about Paris",
 "Travelling south through France into Italy, he came to appreciate Paris's beauty more than he had while there.",
 "","approx","TehranBureau summary of the Travels; route and dates not checked in the translation.")
e("1802-12","Late 1802","range","abutaleb","Constantinople","An Arabic dictionary for Sultan Selim III",
 "At Constantinople he was received by Sultan Selim III, the reforming Ottoman ruler. He presented the sultan with a rare Arabic dictionary. After two years as an exotic in Christian capitals, he was a learned Muslim guest at a Muslim court.",
 "","approx","Banglapedia; Wikipedia. Month not verified.")
e("1803-03","Early 1803 (Shawwal–Dhu al-Hijja 1217)","range","abutaleb","Baghdad, Karbala and Najaf","Pilgrimage to the Shi'i shrines",
 "Crossing Anatolia and Kurdistan overland, he reached Baghdad and made the pilgrimage to the shrines of Husain at Karbala and Ali at Najaf. For a Shi'i traveller this was the religious heart of the journey, after Europe.",
 "","approx","Live History India; Wikipedia (Karbala, Najaf). Dates approximate.")
e("1803-06","Mid-1803","range","abutaleb","Basra, the Gulf and Bombay","By sea from the Gulf to Bombay",
 "From Basra he took ship down the Gulf and across to Bombay, back within the Company's India, before the last passage round to Bengal.",
 "","approx","Live History India (return via Bombay).")
e("1803-08-04","Thursday 4 August 1803 (≈15 Rabi' al-Thani 1218 AH)","day","abutaleb","Calcutta","Home after four and a half years",
 "He landed at Calcutta four and a half years after sailing. He came home with notes, poems and opinions but no post. He began writing up his journey for readers in Hindustan, to tell them about European arts, sciences and manners that he thought they badly needed to know.",
 "","verified","Banglapedia (4 August 1803); Iranica (mid-1803).")
e("1804","1219 AH (1804–05)","year","abutaleb","Calcutta and Lucknow","Writing the book, and the twelve defects",
 "He completed the Masir-i Talibi. Its London chapters close with a chapter on the virtues and defects of the English. He counts twelve defects, among them want of religious faith, pride and insolence, love of money and of ease, irritability, and time wasted in eating, drinking and dressing. A matching account of their virtues sits beside the list.",
 "","approx","Iranica (completed 1219/1804–05); Sen (2008) on the list of twelve; 'virtues' side summarised from the chapter title.")
e("1807","1807","year","abutaleb","London (published in his absence)","His poems printed in London",
 "His Persian poems were published in London with English renderings, including a 'Poem in Praise of Miss Julia Burrell', a young lady of his London acquaintance. It was a tribute from a guest now back in Lucknow. Williamson would soon mock it.",
 "","approx","Iranica (London 1807 edition of his divan); Broadview Appendix A.1.")
e("1810-05","May 1810","month","stewart","Hertford","The translator's preface",
 "Stewart dated his dedication and preface in May 1810. He presented the book as the remarks of an 'intelligent Foreigner' on English society, worth a liberal curiosity precisely because the writer was neither flatterer nor enemy. He also smoothed and abridged the text he was introducing.",
 "intelligent Foreigner","verified","BnF record (preface dated May 1810); preface p. xxxiii as cited by Sen (2008).")
e("1810-05","May 1810","month","bond","Hertford / London","The Mirza's face for the book",
 "William Bond engraved Northcote's portrait of Mirza Abu Taleb Khan, and the print was published by 'Major Stewart, Hertford'. That was the translator himself, in the month of his preface. Readers of the Travels met the author first as London had painted him.",
 "","verified","Rijksmuseum print records (Bond after Northcote, dated 1810-05, publisher Major Stewart, Hertford).")
e("1810","1810","year","williamson","London","'Ingratitude and vanity': the shipmate's verdict",
 "Williamson devoted a section of his Vade-Mecum to the Mirza. He wrote that the Mirza was at first received 'as a general and prince' on the strength of an empty title from the Nawab Vizier's court. In his paltry lodgings in Gresse Street, Williamson said, he boasted of nightly visits from peeresses and ungratefully named them. Williamson suggested he may have been deceived by low women and judged that vanity was his motto.",
 "where he was at first received as a general and prince","verified","Williamson, East India Vade-Mecum (1810) i.503–504, via Pritchett's notes.")
e("1810","1810","year","williamson","London","Mocking the poems as hyperbole",
 "Williamson turned to the Mirza's Persian verses published in England for a young lady and ridiculed them as rhodomontade. He mocked their astronomical and bodily imagery. He used them as proof of the exaggeration he attributed to Indian poets in general, a stereotype stated outright.",
 "","verified","Williamson, East India Vade-Mecum (1810) i.506–508, via Pritchett's notes.")
e("1810-08","August 1810","month","quarterly","London","The Quarterly reviews the Travels",
 "The Quarterly Review gave the translation a full review within months of publication. The visitor London had treated as a prince was now judged as an author.",
 "","approx","Broadview edition, Appendix B.1 (Quarterly Review, August 1810). Content not checked in the original.")
e("1811-08","August 1811","month","eclectic","London","The Eclectic Review takes its turn",
 "The Dissenting Eclectic Review reviewed the Travels a year later, when the book was circulating more widely and appearing in French. A Muslim's charge that the English lacked religious faith landed in a journal devoted to that very question.",
 "","approx","Broadview edition, Appendix B.2 (Eclectic Review, August 1811). Content not checked in the original.")
e("1814","1814","year","stewart","Hertford / London","A second edition, and the Persian original ordered printed",
 "Stewart brought out a second edition. Its advertisement noted that the government of Bengal had ordered the Persian original printed, which was done at Calcutta in 1812, and that French and German versions had appeared. The Mirza, dead since 1805–06, now had readers in four languages.",
 "","approx","Sen (2008) on the 1814 advertisement; Iranica (Calcutta 1812 Persian edition); QNL record of French 1811 edition.")

