d["convergences"]=[
 {"date":"1799-02-07","label":"Sailing from Bengal","eyes":["abutaleb","williamson"],"note":"The Mirza tells the departure as the start of a voyage of discovery with a kind friend. Williamson, by his own later claim a passenger in the same ship, uses that shared voyage eleven years on as the basis for a hostile character sketch."},
 {"date":"1800-07","label":"Summer 1800: the season of the Persian Prince","eyes":["abutaleb","morningpost","williamson"],"note":"Vauxhall crowds and gala breakfasts made him a prince. He says the title was given, not claimed, while the society press reported the grand occasions of the Whig world he moved in. Williamson later insisted the rank was 'an empty title' from the Nawab Vizier's court."},
 {"date":"1800-11-10","label":"The Lord Mayor's Feast at Guildhall","eyes":["abutaleb","oracle"],"note":"The City's paper reported the feast at which Nelson was the hero. The Mirza's account places him near Nelson and reads the bows aimed at him as a misunderstanding about rank, a detail of the evening that only the guest recorded."},
 {"date":"1801","label":"The 'Vindication' in print","eyes":["abutaleb","asiaticreg"],"note":"A drawing-room argument became a published text. The Mirza wrote to correct his hostesses, and the Register gave the rebuttal English print among Company documents. The essay stated the European view in its own first lines, then answered it point by point."},
 {"date":"1801","label":"The portrait","eyes":["northcote","abutaleb","bond"],"note":"Northcote painted the sitter London had made famous. In 1810 Bond's engraving of that portrait, published by Stewart, became the face of the book, so the hosts' image of the traveller introduced the traveller's own judgement of them."},
 {"date":"1810","label":"1810: the reckoning in print","eyes":["stewart","williamson","bond","quarterly"],"note":"In one year the Mirza came back to London in four forms. Stewart's preface welcomed an intelligent foreigner. Bond's print showed the prince's face. Williamson's Vade-Mecum mocked his vanity and poetry, and the Quarterly reviewed the book. The same man was offered to British readers as a sage and as a fraud."}
]
