d["threads_out"]=[
 {"to":"mai-1774","note":"James Northcote, who painted Abu Taleb around 1801, had been Reynolds's pupil and assistant in the 1770s, when Reynolds painted Mai (Omai), London's earlier celebrated visitor from beyond Europe."},
 {"to":"tahtawi-1826","note":"Silvestre de Sacy, whom Abu Taleb met in Paris in 1802, was a generation later the senior orientalist who read and assessed Rifa'a al-Tahtawi's account of Paris. That makes him a link between two Muslim travellers' views of France."},
 {"to":"naser-1873","note":"Later Persian readers knew the Masir-i Talibi, and a modern account claims it stirred the interest of Qajar shahs in seeing Europe. Naser al-Din Shah's European tours continued the Persian-language tradition of travel writing about the West that Abu Taleb helped begin."},
 {"to":"v1:french-rev","note":"Abu Taleb saw Paris in 1802, in the Peace of Amiens, under the Consulate. He was invited to meet Talleyrand and Bonaparte and found the Louvre filled with works taken in the Revolutionary wars."}
]
